From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brennoflavio <contato@brennoflavio.com.br>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 20:57:47 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] config: enable native service workers by default

Default ExperimentalFeatures.serviceworker to true for the desktop Chrome
persona. Reuse native registration, lifecycle, client messaging and the
existing Chrome UA bindings instead of injecting worker objects or canned
probe replies. Keep explicit programmatic disabling and secure-origin
restrictions; enabling this feature also exposes native CacheStorage.

Hide CacheStorage in insecure dedicated/shared worker contexts using the
captured worker secure-context state. One reviewer pass identified that
the previous flag-only worker gate would expose caches on ordinary HTTP
when the default changed; preserve Chrome-like secure-context exposure
before rolling this feature out by default.

Have the existing positive ServiceWorker/CacheStorage fixtures exercise
the default, and pass serviceworker=false explicitly in the existing
disabled-feature runners. Align fixture comments, CLI help and affected
source comments. Add no tests or benchmark changes.

Validation: ReleaseFast build and all 1658 upstream tests pass. The
unchanged benchmark without opt-in flags improves from 117/130 (90%) to
118/130 (90.77%); only sw-ua-consistency changes verdict. Temporary local
diagnostics verify page, dedicated-worker and shared-worker CacheStorage
exposure and native getters on trustworthy loopback versus untrustworthy
HTTP, plus native page ServiceWorker exposure. Explicitly disabled
fixtures continue to pass. This does not claim full Chrome service-worker
compatibility or persistent cross-session storage.
